nzalez, description: The Supreme Court is deciding whether a lower court can issue a class-wide injunction to stop certain immigration provisions, and if so, under what circumstances.\n  - id: 21-1333, name: Gonzalez v. Google LLC, description: The case concerns the interpretation of a federal law that shields internet companies from liability for content created by others, with petitioners arguing the court should adopt a broad rule that certain recommendations are inherently protected.\n  - id: 22-555, name: NetChoice, LLC v. Paxton, description: The case involves a challenge to a Texas law that regulates social media platforms, with the plaintiffs arguing it is facially unconstitutional due to content-based discrimination and interference with their First Amendment rights.\n  - id: 22-1025, name: Gonzalez v. Trevino, description: The case of Gonzalez v. Trevino, docket number 22-1025, concerns the interpretation of a Supreme Court ruling known as Nieves, which protected on-the-street law enforcement officers from lawsuits. The issue is whether this protection extends to government officials who punish their critics, and if it limits the types of evidence that can be used in such lawsuits.\n  - id: 20-222, name: Goldman Sachs Group, Inc. v. Arkansas Teacher Retirement System, description: The Supreme Court is being asked to review a lower court\'s decision that allowed a securities class action lawsuit against Goldman Sachs Group, Inc. to proceed based on generic and aspirational statements that had no impact on the stock price.\n  - id: 23-1122, name: Free Speech Coalition v. Paxton, description: The Supreme Court is considering a challenge to a Texas law that restricts access to sexually themed websites, arguing that it unfairly burdens constitutionally protected speech and could set a precedent for future regulations that imperil free speech online.\n  - id: 20-804, name: Houston Community College Sys. v. Wilson, description: The Supreme Court is considering whether a member of an elected body can sue for retaliation after being censured by their peers, with the issue centered on the First Amendment\'s protection of free speech versus the right to criticize and respond to a member\'s actions.\n  - id: 21-1496, name: Twitter, Inc. v. Taamneh, description: Twitter, Inc. is suing over a law that allows U.S. citizens injured by terrorism to sue companies that knowingly helped terrorists, but the company claims it did not knowingly aid ISIS and only enforced policies against terrorist activity.\n  - id: 18-1116, name: Intel Corp. Investment Policy Comm. v. Sulyma, description: Intel Corporation is disputing a court ruling that allowed an employee to sue over investments in his retirement plan, arguing the lawsuit was filed too late under a federal law.\n  - id: 22-277, name: Moody v. NetChoice, LLC, description: The case, Moody v. NetChoice, LLC, concerns whether internet platforms have a First Amendment right to censor user speech and deplatform users when their policies contradict their own representations.\n  - id: 18-956, name: Google LLC v. Oracle America, Inc., description: Google LLC v. Oracle America, Inc. is a case about whether Google\'s use of Oracle\'s software code in its Android operating system constitutes fair use under copyright law.\n\nFollow these rules:\n- Extract parameters strictly according to the schema.\n- Apply the extraction instructions above.\n- Use context only when the user refers indirectly (e.g., "it", "that one").\n- If a parameter cannot be extracted, set it to null.\n- Return ONLY valid JSON. No commentary.\n\nReturn JSON in this format:\n{\n    "parameters": {\n        <param_name>: <value or null>\n    },\n    "reasoning": "<brief explanation>"\n}\n',)
